meta {
  name: Start sandbox
  type: http
  seq: 50
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/sim/players
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "sessionId": 250,
    "boats": [
      { "deviceId": "AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:01", "speedKn": 14.5 },
      { "deviceId": "AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:02", "speedKn": 13.5 }
    ],
    "timeScale": 50
  }
}

docs {
  201 { sessionId, running, timeScale, startedAt, endedAt, warnings, boats:[{deviceId, runId, speedKn, played, total, startEpoch, finishEpoch, elapsedSec, uploaded, failed, done}] }
  400 { error }   // not simulated, no boats, bad speedKn, or timeScale above MAX_TIME_SCALE (50)
  404 { error }   // session not found
  409 { error }   // a player is already sailing it, or the boats got different guns

  timeScale: 1 = wall clock, 10/25/50 = compressed (todo 66). The player writes the
  tape's own epochs, so the recorded race is the same at any scale - only the wall
  wait changes. A 150-fix tape: 1x ~155 s, 10x ~19 s, 50x ~6.5 s on local+Turso.
  Omitting `boats` sails every boat already in the session (13.5/14.5 kn rotation).

  Todo 63 — the sandbox: the BACKEND sails this session with no device on the
  other end. One engine run per boat, every fix replayed into POST /gps as that
  boat's DeviceId (so it lands in gps_points like a real one), and the run
  uploads at its own finish.

  The session must already exist and be marked with `simulated: true` — the
  web UI's "Simulate" switch on the New-session pane does both. Participants are
  still declared once at creation (todo 55), so `boats` may only name boats
  already in the session; omit it to sail every declared boat at the default
  speeds (13.5, then 14.5 kn).

  Sessions left in the DB on purpose, like the bench: inspect them on the map,
  then sweep with `node tools/prune-db.js --sim-sessions --yes`.

  RAM only, like the scripts: a restart loses the player, and the boot sweep
  abandons whatever it left behind reading scheduled/live.
}
